429: 
430: ## SC.5. Excision, its zero-index correction, and the transfer sign
431: 
432: Take a small seven-ball around the local lifted germ inside
433: \(Z=Y\times D_\rho\); its boundary contains the inner embedded
434: three-sphere \(e\). Remove the normal disc bundle of \(W\) from the
435: remaining ambient cobordism. Attach the product surgery collar
436: \((D^3\times S^1)\times S^2\) on the outer side, as in K7, and the
437: \(D^4\times S^2\) collar on the inner side. The outer closed boundary
438: is the actual physical mapping torus \(P_f\) after fixed-sphere
439: replacement. The inner closed boundary is the surgery manifold
440: \(N_e\) of the actual embedded link, with the same meridian line.
441: The further boundary is the normal sphere bundle over
442: \[
443:  \overline W=W\cup(D^3\times S^1)\cup D^4
444:        =(S^2\times D^2)\cup(D^3\times S^1)
445:        =\partial(D^3\times D^2)\cong S^4.            \tag{SC.16}
446: \]
447: These are the literal boundary identifications from the construction;
448: the two caps are not exchanged by an unspecified change of marking.
449: 
450: The varying outer normalization coordinate also has an explicit cap
451: extension. On the outer parameter circle put
452: \[
453:  A_\rho=\begin{pmatrix}1&-\rho\\1&\rho\end{pmatrix},
454:  \qquad Z=A_\rho\cdot s=\frac{s-\rho}{s+\rho}.
455: \]
456: Represent the closed three-ball by positive semidefinite Hermitian
457: two-by-two matrices \(P\) of trace one. Its boundary consists of the
458: rank-one matrices and is \(\mathbf P^1\). The exact ball map is
459: \[
460:  \mathcal B_\rho(P)
461:        =\frac{A_\rho P A_\rho^*}
462:                     {\operatorname{tr}(A_\rho P A_\rho^*)}.
463:                                                                \tag{SC.16a}
464: \]
465: Its inverse uses \(A_\rho^{-1}\). The denominator is positive on the
466: closed ball, so both maps are smooth there. On its boundary it is
467: exactly the displayed Mobius map. The matrix \(A_\rho\) is periodic
468: on the actual parameter circle, so this ball map closes literally even
469: when the associated projective loop has nontrivial fundamental-group
470: class. The source reparametrization isotopies of (SC.10a) extend as
471: well: extend their time-dependent sphere vector fields over a boundary
472: collar of the ball using a radial cutoff, and integrate them. These
473: extensions are periodic in the parameter. Composing them with
474: (SC.16a) identifies the actual outer cap marking with the product cap
475: in (SC.16). Its normal clutch is carried along, not set to zero.
476: 
477: The actual Euler line extends over both collars. On the outer boundary
478: its class is the positive meridian class, by (SC.14); every possible
479: boundary gauge extends over \(D^3\times S^1\), since restriction is
480: an isomorphism on \(H^1\). On the inner \(S^3\) there is no
481: \(H^1\) gauge obstruction. Both normal bundles extend after choosing
482: their actual boundary framings; any resulting normal Pontryagin class
483: is retained. The extra sphere-bundle boundary has index zero, since its
484: base is (SC.16), \(H^2(S^4)=0\), and \(\sigma(S^4)=0\). The sphere-
485: bundle formula cancels the normal Pontryagin class and gives this zero
486: without a normal-framing assumption.
487: 
488: The orientations are the same two reversals as K7. The complex
